  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through Friday)\nIssued at 102 AM CDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nSome weak upper-level ridging will return across Texas for Sunday \ninto Monday ahead of the approach of the next system. Otherwise, \nwarm and humid conditions with southeasterly surface flow will \ncontinue through Monday.  This will result in unseasonably warm high \ntemperatures in the mid to upper 90s with some low chances (<15%) \nfor brief light showers each afternoon.\n\nA significant and long-awaited pattern shift appears to be likely \nstarting Tuesday.  Models are in fairly good agreement on an upper-\nlevel trough moving out of California and into Texas on Tuesday and \nWednesday. At the surface a fairly strong Pacific-based cold front \nwill push southeastward into northwest Texas Wednesday through \nThursday. This could potentially bring more seasonal temperatures \ninto west central Texas for Wednesday and Thursday. However, the \nforecast will hinge significantly on the strength of this front and \nhow far south this feature makes it into Texas. The latest ensemble \nspread of high temperatures shows a range from the upper 70s to \naround 90 degrees with a mean in the low 80s. The other uncertainty \nis rainfall chances, timing and potentially amounts.  These will \ndepend heavily on the path of Hurricane Polo, which is currently \nlocated just south of Baja California. Global models continue to \nshow good agreement on bringing Polo into northwest Mexico on \nTuesday. Assuming this scenario plays out Polo's remnants would \nmerge with the aforementioned trough and cold front as they push \ninto Texas. Model blends currently show at least medium (40-60%) \nchances for rain for late Tuesday through Thursday so confidence is \nincreasing on at least some rainfall for west central Texas.\nHowever, there is quite a bit of uncertainty on where the heaviest \nrain could occur. This would depend on the path of Polo's remnants, \nhow much moisture there there is, and where the front stalls out.",
